# 27. Frontend

The frontend should be a modern web application.

The exact framework can follow the developer's existing project setup. If no existing frontend framework is mandated, React is recommended.

Primary screens:

```text
/                         Upload Dashboard
/case/{id}                Extraction Results
/case/{id}/review         Document Review
```

---

# 28. Processing Architecture

For the MVP, processing can initially be synchronous/asynchronous depending on document size.

For multiple documents, the preferred model is:

```text
Upload
 ↓
Create processing jobs
 ↓
Process documents independently
 ↓
Update status
 ↓
Frontend polls/subscribes to status
 ↓
Display completed results
```

This allows one failed document to fail independently without blocking all other documents.

Example:

```text
PAN.pdf          ✓ SUCCESS
Aadhaar.pdf      ✓ SUCCESS
GST.pdf          ✓ SUCCESS
LOA.pdf          ⚠ PARTIAL
SVR.docx         ✓ SUCCESS
Cheque.jpg       ❌ FAILED
```

The user should still be able to review the successful documents.

---

# 29. Security Requirements

Because KYC documents contain sensitive personal/business information, security must be treated as a core requirement even in the MVP.

The system should:

* Use HTTPS.
* Avoid logging document contents.
* Avoid logging PAN/Aadhaar values unnecessarily.
* Use temporary signed/private document access.
* Restrict storage bucket access.
* Never expose storage buckets publicly.
* Delete temporary source documents after expiration.
* Avoid putting document contents into URLs.
* Avoid storing credentials in source code.
* Use environment variables/secrets management.
* Validate uploaded file types.
* Limit upload sizes.

The application should also avoid storing sensitive document content in application logs.

---

# 30. Data Deletion

Deletion must occur at the storage level.

The cleanup mechanism should delete:

```text
Original uploaded files
Temporary rendered files
Temporary OCR artifacts
```

after expiration.

Firestore records may either:

1. Be deleted with the document, or
2. Be retained temporarily as non-sensitive processing metadata.

The exact production retention policy should be configurable.

The MVP should at minimum ensure that the original uploaded document itself is deleted automatically.

---

# 31. Error Model

Backend errors should be structured.

Example:

```json
{
  "code": "OCR_LOW_CONFIDENCE",
  "message": "The document could not be read reliably.",
  "document_id": "doc_123",
  "page": 1,
  "retryable": true
}
```

Suggested error codes:

```text
UNSUPPORTED_FILE_TYPE
FILE_TOO_LARGE
FILE_CORRUPTED
PDF_PARSE_FAILED
DOCX_PARSE_FAILED
OCR_FAILED
OCR_LOW_CONFIDENCE
NO_TEXT_DETECTED
PARTIAL_EXTRACTION
PROCESSING_TIMEOUT
STORAGE_ERROR
UNKNOWN_EXTRACTION_ERROR
```

---

# 32. Observability

The system should log processing metadata, not sensitive document contents.

Useful logs:

```text
case_id
document_id
filename
processing_start
processing_end
extractor_used
status
error_code
processing_duration
```

Avoid:

```text
PAN value
Aadhaar number
full document text
personal address
```

---

# 33. Non-Functional Requirements

## Performance

For normal KYC documents, the system should provide processing feedback quickly.

The UI should not appear frozen while documents are being processed.

Example:

```text
PAN.pdf       Processing...
GST.pdf       Processing...
COI.pdf       ✓ Complete
SVR.docx      Processing...
```

## Reliability

A failure in one document must not crash the entire case.

## Scalability

The architecture should support multiple users uploading cases concurrently.

## Maintainability

OCR/extraction providers should be replaceable.

## Traceability

Every extracted field must be traceable back to its source document.

## Privacy

Uploaded source documents should be temporary by default.

---

# 34. Phase 1 Acceptance Criteria

Phase 1 will be considered successful when a user can:

### Upload

* Upload 8–15 documents in one operation.
* Upload mixed supported file types.
* See upload status.

### Process

* Extract text from digital PDFs.
* Extract text from DOCX.
* OCR scanned PDFs/images where possible.
* Process documents independently.

### Review

* See all extracted information in one page.
* See which document produced each value.
* See page numbers.
* Open the source document.
* Review source and extracted information side-by-side.

### Failure Handling

* Clearly see failed documents.
* See why extraction failed.
* Distinguish partial extraction from complete failure.
* Retry/re-upload a failed document.

### Temporary Storage

* Source files remain available during the review period.
* Source files are automatically deleted after the configured retention period.
* The UI handles expired documents gracefully.

---

# 35. MVP Development Sequence

## Step 1 — Project Setup

Create:

```text
FastAPI backend
Frontend
Firebase project
Firestore
Cloud Storage
Environment configuration
```

---

## Step 2 — Upload System

Implement:

```text
Create case
Upload multiple files
Validate files
Store temporary files
Create Firestore metadata
```

---

## Step 3 — Basic PDF Extraction

First implement digital PDF text extraction.

Do NOT start with sophisticated ML.

Goal:

```text
PAN.pdf
 ↓
Text
 ↓
Page-level results
```

---

## Step 4 — DOCX Extraction

Add DOCX support.

---

## Step 5 — OCR

Add OCR fallback:

```text
PDF has text?
    │
    ├── YES → extract text
    │
    └── NO → OCR
```

---

## Step 6 — Extraction Failure System

Implement:

```text
SUCCESS
PARTIAL
FAILED
UNSUPPORTED
```

before adding advanced information extraction.

This is important because failure visibility is a core product requirement.

---

## Step 7 — Structured Information Extraction

Initially support a small set of common fields:

```text
Name
PAN
GSTIN
Address
Company Name
Document Number
Date
```

The extraction architecture should allow more fields to be added later.

---

## Step 8 — Consolidated Results Page

Build the first page showing all extracted information.

---

## Step 9 — Document Review Page

Build:

```text
Document viewer | Extracted information
```

---

## Step 10 — Provenance Linking

Connect:

```text
Extracted value
      ↓
Document
      ↓
Page
      ↓
Source location
```

---

## Step 11 — Automatic Deletion

Implement configurable expiration:

```env
DOCUMENT_RETENTION_MINUTES=10
```

Use server-side/storage-side cleanup.

---

## Step 12 — Testing

Create test cases for:

1. Digital PDF
2. Scanned PDF
3. Clear image
4. Blurry image
5. Corrupted PDF
6. Unsupported file
7. DOCX
8. Multi-page PDF
9. Multiple simultaneous uploads
10. Partial OCR
11. Empty document
12. Expired document

---

# 36. Future Phases

After Phase 1 works reliably:

## Phase 2 — Document Classification

Automatically identify:

```text
PAN
Aadhaar
GST
COI
MOA/AOA
LOA
BO
SVR
RBI License
etc.
```

## Phase 3 — KYC Requirement Engine

Use case type + organization type to determine expected documents.

Example:

```text
Entity:
Corporate

Type:
Private Limited

Required:
COI
MOA/AOA
Company PAN
GST
BO
Annexure L1
LOA
SVR
Cluster Head Approval
```

The supplied requirements demonstrate why this needs to be rule-driven rather than hard-coded to one universal checklist.

## Phase 4 — Cross-Document Comparison

Compare information across documents:

```text
PAN
    ↓
Company Name

GST
    ↓
Legal Name

COI
    ↓
Company Name
```

Flag potential mismatches.

## Phase 5 — KYC Assistance

Generate:

```text
Complete
Incomplete
Potential mismatch
Missing document
Manual review required
```

## Phase 6 — Remarks Assistance

Eventually assist the employee in generating standardized remarks based on verified extraction results.

---

# 37. Design Principles

The implementation should follow these principles.

### 1. Extraction before validation

First determine:

> What does the document say?

Only later determine:

> Is the information valid?

### 2. Never hide uncertainty

If extraction is unreliable, explicitly tell the user.

### 3. Every value needs provenance

Never create an extracted field without knowing where it came from when the source can be determined.

### 4. Documents are temporary

Do not build the Phase 1 architecture around permanent document storage.

### 5. One document failure must not stop the case

Documents are processed independently.

### 6. Replaceable extraction engines

OCR/document extraction technology must be abstracted behind interfaces.

### 7. Human review remains authoritative

Phase 1 assists the employee. It does not make the KYC decision.

---

# 38. Definition of Done

Phase 1 is complete when the following workflow works end-to-end:

```text
User
 │
 │ Uploads 8–15 documents
 ▼
FastAPI
 │
 ▼
Temporary Cloud Storage
 │
 ▼
Document Processing
 │
 ├── Digital text extraction
 │
 └── OCR fallback
 │
 ▼
Extraction Results
 │
 ▼
Firestore
 │
 ▼
Frontend
 │
 ├── Consolidated Information Page
 │
 └── Document Review Page
 │
 ▼
Source-linked review
 │
 ▼
Temporary retention expires
 │
 ▼
Original uploaded files deleted
```

The final user experience should reduce the need to manually switch between document explorer, spreadsheets, and individual documents simply to locate and compare information.

The primary Phase 1 success metric is therefore:

> **A user can upload an entire KYC document set once and inspect the extracted information and its source documents from a single application.**
